<p>The story her admirers liked to tell about her was not about genius. It was about a bell. As a young woman in an unglamorous seaside town in Kent, Elizabeth Carter tied a bell to the frame of her bedstead so that it would wake her before daylight — and then she got up and learned Greek. It is the kind of detail that flatters the teller, and Virginia Woolf, writing two centuries later, gave it the line that kept it alive: she called for "homage to the robust shade of Eliza Carter — the valiant old woman who tied a bell to her bedstead in order that she might wake early and learn Greek."</p>

<p>What makes Carter worth studying now is not that she was gifted. By the account of the person who knew her earliest, she was nearly given up on. Her story is a case study in the opposite of the talent myth: a learner who was slow at the start, who could not absorb grammar as rules, and who built a method out of her own stubbornness instead — then used it to translate a Stoic philosopher for the English-speaking world for the next hundred and fifty years.</p>

<h2>Deal, Kent, 1717: The Curate's Daughter</h2>

<p>Elizabeth Carter was born on 16 December 1717 in Deal, on the Kent coast, the eldest child of the Reverend Nicolas Carter, perpetual curate of the town, and his first wife, Margaret. Her mother died when Elizabeth was ten. The redbrick family house still stands at the corner of South Street and Middle Street, a short walk from the sea.</p>

<p>Her father was a learned man with a large family and a modest living, and rather than pay for schooling he took on the education of his children himself — teaching them, in the Latin and Greek languages, at the kitchen table. To this he added Hebrew for his eldest daughter. This is the part of the "self-taught" legend that needs correcting honestly: Carter did not teach herself from nothing. She had a teacher, and he was a good one. What she did not have was any institution, any school, any university, or any expectation that a girl from a clerical family in a small port town might become one of the finest classicists in the country. Everything beyond her father's first lessons, she built herself.</p>

<h2>The Child Who Was Almost Given Up On</h2>

<p>Here is the sentence most biographies skip past. According to the early account of her life, her father was so discouraged by how slowly his eldest daughter understood her lessons that he nearly despaired of ever making her a scholar, and would have given up — but for one thing: her <em>resolute perseverance</em>. She struggled, and she did not stop struggling.</p>

<p>It is worth sitting with, because it dismantles the story most people tell themselves about learning a language. Carter was not a prodigy who found Greek easy. She was the slow student in her own family, the one the teacher worried about, and she out-learned every faster sibling and nearly everyone she met. The difference was not aptitude. It was the willingness to keep going at the exact point where the discouragement kicked in — and then to build a routine sturdy enough to carry her through the years it would take.</p>

<h2>A Method of Her Own: Deduced From the Literature, Not the Grammar Book</h2>

<p>The most interesting fact about how Carter learned is how she handled grammar. She "could never acquire grammar as a rudimentary theory" — the abstract tables, the declensions recited as rules, the way most modern learners are told to begin. Instead, having reached real proficiency in Greek and Latin, she "deduced the principles from the literature." She learned the grammar <em>backwards</em>: by reading enough real text that the patterns revealed themselves.</p>

<p>This is precisely the approach that modern research on acquisition keeps rediscovering. Beginners are usually handed the rulebook first, but the learners who reach the highest levels tend to be the ones who spend enormous time inside real language and let the rules crystallise from experience. Carter arrived at the same conclusion three centuries early, not from theory but from necessity, because rules-as-theory simply did not stick for her. She found the route her particular mind could take, and then took it for decades.</p>

<h2>Nine Languages and a Year in Canterbury</h2>

<p>She was not finished with Latin, Greek and Hebrew. On her father's instruction she was sent to board for a year with the family of a refugee minister, M. Le Seur, in Canterbury, to learn French — where she came to understand it and speak it fluently. From there she added Italian, Spanish, German and Portuguese, working at them the way she worked at everything: early in the morning and late into the night. Late in life she took up enough Arabic to read it without a dictionary.</p>

<p>The physical cost was real. Being, as the record puts it, naturally heavy, and determined to stay awake as long as possible, she took to snuff and chewed green tea to hold off sleep, and she developed intense headaches from over-application that troubled her for the rest of her life. She also studied astronomy and ancient geography, learned to play the spinnet and the German flute, drew reasonably well, and — a detail the age cared about — could not neglect the Greek and the pudding alike. To counter the effects of too much study she walked long distances and went to parties.</p>

<p>By sixteen, writing under the name <em>Eliza</em>, she had pieces in Edward Cave's <em>Gentleman's Magazine</em>. At twenty she had met Samuel Johnson. Her first anonymous collection of poems appeared in 1738, the same year she translated the Swiss philosopher Jean Pierre de Crousaz, and soon after she rendered into English the Italian scientist Francesco Algarotti's <em>Newtonianismo per le dame</em>.</p>

<h2>1749: The Epictetus Translation</h2>

<p>Her reputation rests on a single, enormous project. Early in 1749 she began translating <em>All the Works of Epictetus, Which are Now Extant</em> — the collected writings of the first-century Stoic philosopher — from the Greek. She submitted the work sheet by sheet for revision to her friend Thomas Secker, then Bishop of Oxford and later Archbishop of Canterbury. She finished the <em>Discourses</em> in December 1752 and, at his suggestion, added the <em>Enchiridion</em> and the fragments, with an introduction and notes.</p>

<p>It was published in 1758 by subscription — the money raised by her wealthy and well-connected friends — and it was the first English translation of the known works of Epictetus. It brought her a clear profit of £1,000, went through three editions, and held its place as the standard English version for generations. When she completed it, she was at the same time preparing her youngest brother for the University of Cambridge — a daughter of the household giving a son his tuition.</p>

<h2>The Bluestockings and the Nine Living Muses</h2>

<p>Carter belonged to the circle of women who gathered around Elizabeth Montagu and became known as the Bluestockings — a set of educated, independent-minded women who took seriously the life of the mind at a time when it was not their allotted role. Her closest friends were Montagu, Hester Chapone, Hannah More, Mary Delany and Catherine Talbot, with whom she exchanged letters for decades, and whose papers she later edited and published after Talbot's death in 1770.</p>

<p>She was placed among the great women of her age in Richard Samuel's painting <em>The Nine Living Muses of Great Britain</em> (1779). She was, noted observers agreed, the most seriously learned of them all. Fanny Burney wrote of her in 1780: "a really noble-looking woman; I never saw age so graceful in the female sex yet; her whole face seems to beam with goodness, piety, and philanthropy." Another diarist was less kind, which is as good a sign as any that she was a real person and not a plaster saint.</p>

<p>She never married. When her father raised the question of a match more than once, she declined, wanting to keep her independence. It was a decision that bought her the thing she valued most: her own hours, and her own books. She adopted the older courtesy title "Mrs." anyway, for the standing it carried.</p>

<h2>The Pudding and the Poem</h2>

<p>Samuel Johnson — no easy man to impress — left the comment that did most to fix her in popular memory, and it captures exactly the double standard she lived inside:</p>

<blockquote>My old friend, Mrs. Carter, could make a pudding as well as translate Epictetus from the Greek, and work a handkerchief as well as compose a poem. — <strong>Samuel Johnson</strong></blockquote>

<p>Read it once as praise and it is warm. Read it twice and you hear the assumption underneath: that the wonder was not that she translated a philosopher, but that a woman who could translate a philosopher could also make a pudding. She spent a writing life being praised in the shape of a surprise, and she went on working through it.</p>

<p>It was a habit she kept to the end. In 1796 she fell gravely ill and never fully recovered, but she continued visiting the poor and founding and running charitable institutions. Increasing deafness narrowed her conversation. Elizabeth Montagu, her friend of forty-four years, died in 1800 at eighty. On 19 February 1806, after a long decline, Elizabeth Carter died at her lodgings in Clarges Street, London. She was eighty-eight.</p>

<h2>What Her Method Actually Teaches</h2>

<p>Strip away the portrait of the learned old woman and what remains is a working method that any language learner can copy today:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Start slow and do not stop there.</strong> She was the slowest of her father's pupils and became the most accomplished. Early difficulty predicted nothing; persistence predicted everything.</li>
  <li><strong>Learn grammar from the text, not only from the table.</strong> She could not absorb rules as theory and deduced them from literature instead — a route now well supported by research on acquisition.</li>
  <li><strong>Build a routine you can keep.</strong> A bell on the bedstead is not glamour. It is the recognition that the work has to happen on schedule, not on inspiration.</li>
  <li><strong>Read the hard things in the original.</strong> Her whole career was built on refusing to wait for a translation and making one herself when the language she wanted was Greek.</li>
  <li><strong>Do your own measuring.</strong> She was judged slow and turned out to be the one who finished. Institutions and even fathers are poor judges of what a determined learner will do over thirty years.</li>
</ul>

<p>There is a quiet lesson in the fact that the woman Johnson praised for pudding and poem never had the vote, the degree, or the seat, and still set the English text of Epictetus for a century and a half. She could not change the rules of her world, so she read her way around them in nine languages. The bell she tied to her bed is the whole method in miniature: decide that the learning will happen before the day can talk you out of it — and then get up and do it.</p>

